<p>A 25-year-old woman from Barcelona, Noelia Castillo Ramos, died by legal euthanasia on Thursday at a care facility in Sant Pere de Ribes, near Barcelona, after Spanish courts rejected last-minute efforts by her father and a Christian legal group to stop the procedure. Multiple reports say Castillo had been paraplegic since a suicide attempt in 2022, lived with constant pain, and had received approval under Spain’s 2021 euthanasia law before the case became the country’s first such dispute to reach the courts. The case drew national attention in Spain and wider interest across Europe because it tested how far an adult patient’s right to assisted death extends when family members object. Courts ultimately upheld Castillo’s request, including a favorable ruling from the European Court of Human Rights earlier in the week, reinforcing Spain’s legal framework for assisted dying while renewing debate over mental health, disability, family authority, and end-of-life autonomy.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/3Ju4Y-8xPNgHz2fPb6OTa_isYhsYUe_M06rCAmnHKR2khjhYIia8Y3u88hISkWn347Uc9MR8n-PglK5mLQEv8VEErPP0rbvuoc8uZJnRhyyMbkP66sUp7zYbJMPKSH4sXgKszRLLGWwywg0jseFqGmwYHPZqEJF-rqOdToHa6XFlolhQfzPxBSqTkPEt9XKnKE-rfcXv9t3T9rokv6WVk3uEquy4Cg2nMvYNJdQ' alt='A still from Noelia Castillo&#x27;s Antena 3 interview on March 24.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Television interview: Before her death, Castillo gave an interview to Spanish television and described deep strain with her family, a rare public step in a case that had become a national legal test.</li><li>Family history: RT reported that Castillo spent part of her childhood in shelters, offering more context for the family breakdown that later came into view during the legal fight.</li><li>Final timing: EL PAÍS said the euthanasia was scheduled for 6 p.m. local time, and SCMP reported that she died shortly afterward.</li><li>Father’s reaction: RT said her father argued that she needed help rather than assisted death, and that her death would cause him great suffering despite their complicated relationship.</li></ul><blockquote>I can’t take this family anymore - Noelia Castillo Ramos</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Noelia Castillo Ramos: Castillo said her daily life had become unbearable and pursued assisted death over an extended period despite repeated legal delays, framing the request as her own settled choice. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c7vqdd23y0vo'>BBC</a>)</li><li>Her father: For about two years, her father tried to block the euthanasia, arguing that his daughter needed support and treatment rather than assisted death. (<a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/spanish-woman-25-dies-legal-euthanasia-case-drew-131445778'>ABC News</a>)</li><li>Abogados Cristianos: The Christian Lawyers group backed the father’s final emergency application and helped lead the legal campaign against the procedure for roughly a year and a half. (<a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-03-26/noelia-castillo-the-young-woman-who-fought-her-parents-for-her-right-to-die-i-cant-take-this-family-anymore.html'>EL PAÍS</a>)</li><li>Spanish and European courts: Judges and the European Court of Human Rights allowed the euthanasia to go forward, signaling that an adult patient’s approved request could outweigh family objections under the law. (<a href='https://edition.cnn.com/2026/03/26/europe/euthanasia-spain-noelia-castillo-latam-intl'>CNN</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/news/636310-they-call-it-mercy/'>Should society help you to die? The EU now has a case to answer</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-03-26/noelia-castillo-the-young-woman-who-fought-her-parents-for-her-right-to-die-i-cant-take-this-family-anymore.html'>Noelia Castillo, the young woman who fought her parents for her right to die: ‘I can’t take this family anymore’</a> - elpais.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.co